{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310731,"nonce":"oxHHX3J7qjjCFXM6+8IDrrrhHxc=","title":"RESOLVED 2026-06-29 (d3c08ccc): test_copy/test_copy_clone_identity ~70% exit-133 flake was a USE-AFTER-FREE in @:derive(Copy) `var b = a` ...","message":"**RESOLVED 2026-06-29, commit d3c08ccc.** The ~70% intermittent exit-133\n(SIGTRAP / EXC_BREAKPOINT) on `test_copy.hx` and `test_copy_clone_identity.hx`\nwas a **use-after-free**, found by lldb (`brk #0x1` in\n`libsystem_malloc _xzm_xzone_malloc_freelist_outlined` = corrupted free list)\n+ a 4-agent workflow that triple-corroborated the root cause from the MIR.\n\n**Root cause:** the HIR→MIR Let lowering's ownership-transfer/alias block\n(`hir_to_mir.rs` ~4800, meant for moving a heap value `var current = n1`)\nran UNCONDITIONALLY for a Variable-RHS `var b = a`. For a `@:derive(Copy)`\nclass, `emit_shallow_copy` had ALREADY made an independent copy `$16` and\nregistered `b` to own it; the transfer block then called\n`register_owned_value(b, a_id)` while `b` already owned `$16`, so\n`register_owned_value`'s reassignment branch emitted `build_free($16)`\nmid-function. The next `b.x = 42` / `trace(b.x)` touched freed memory (UAF\nwrite), and a later malloc (`new Point(10,20)`) walked the corrupted free\nlist and trapped. Intermittent because the trap depends on whether the next\nmalloc revisits the corrupted bucket.\n\n**Fix:** guard the transfer block with `\u0026\u0026 copy_class_sym.is_none()`\n(skip it when the RHS was deep-copied), mirroring the Assign path's existing\n`rhs_was_copied` guard. Copy independence preserved; the copy lives its full\nscope. Verified: test_copy `--no-cache` 40/40 (was ~6/20), clone 20/20,\nvalues 3/42/3/10, full suite 153/153.\n\n**My prior theory in this file was WRONG** — I had attributed the flake to\n\"W0020 / HashMap-iteration-order trap-stub-stranding\". It was a genuine UAF.\nLesson reinforced: an intermittent exit-133 is often heap corruption — reach\nfor lldb + libgmalloc to find the *corrupting op*, not just the trap site\n(the malloc-time trap names the victim, not the culprit). The N≥40 A/B rule\nstill held (it correctly classified it as pre-existing, not my feature), but\n\"pre-existing flake, unfixable\" was the wrong conclusion — it had a fixable\nroot cause. See also [[feedback_debug_at_head_not_timetravel]].\n\n**Still true:** test_exception's exit-133 in the cumulative-cache suite IS a\nseparate F6 cumulative-cache flake (passes `--no-cache`); see\n[[bugs_blade_mixed_cache_coherence]]. Don't lump it with this (now-fixed) one.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_test_copy_clone_identity_flaky` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}